Pravah MVP1 Tasks:
    Confirm MVP1 scope [Project Manager]:
        ✔ Confirm Go is the primary backend and mark the Node implementation as legacy or remove it from the supported runtime.
        ✔ Approve the signal-only payload contract: event name, resource ID, stream ID, timestamp, and event ID.
        ✔ Define Docker Compose deployment, MongoDB 9, internal networking, and production reverse-proxy HTTPS strategy.
        ✔ Define MVP limits for 100 concurrent connections, 50 publications/second, configurable retention and payload limits, and sub-second delivery.
        ✔ Document deferred Phase 2 features such as hosted infrastructure, horizontal scaling, and enterprise analytics.
    Establish application security [Backend]:
        ✔ Restrict application registration so a public caller cannot create unlimited API keys.
        ✔ Validate stream names, event names, identifiers, request bodies, and configured operational limits.
        ✔ Prevent the publish API from accepting or retaining arbitrary metadata in the signal-only path.
        ✔ Add secure production cookie settings controlled through environment configuration.
        ✔ Configure CORS from an environment allowlist instead of allowing every origin with credentials.
        ✔ Add request-scoped database contexts and protect tenant configuration with a signing secret.
        ✔ Define and implement signed, short-lived per-stream subscription authorization for supported stream types.
        ✔ Isolate persisted events, live clients, and replay by tenant application ID.
        ✔ Redact subscription tokens from request logs.
        ✔ Avoid returning database errors or other internal implementation details in API responses.
    Implement reliable event delivery [Backend]:
        ✔ Add expiration time to persisted events and create a MongoDB TTL index for configurable retention.
        ✔ Emit standards-compliant SSE `id:`, `event:`, and `data:` fields.
        ✔ Send persisted event IDs to clients and preserve those IDs for reconnection.
        ✔ Read the `Last-Event-ID` request header and replay missed events in order before live delivery.
        ✔ Limit replay queries by the event retention window and the client's authorized streams.
        ✔ Ensure replay and live delivery cannot duplicate or skip signals during subscription startup.
        ✔ Allow live publishing to continue when persistence is unavailable, with explicit degraded-mode signaling.
        ✔ Return health status that reflects database availability without misreporting a degraded service as healthy.
    Harden connection management [Backend]:
        ✔ Remove disconnected clients from the registry on every shutdown and error path.
        ✔ Add configurable heartbeat, client-buffer, stream, replay, and request-size limits.
        ✔ Close clients whose buffers are full instead of silently dropping events.
        ✔ Add structured logs for connection open/close, authentication failures, publish failures, replay, and dropped delivery.
        ✔ Add metrics for active connections, publications, publication latency, replay count, and delivery failures.
        ✔ Verify concurrent publish and client removal are safe under load.
    Complete the browser SDK [SDK]:
        ✔ Remove the circular dependency between the core SDK and React hooks.
        ✔ Expose connection states for initializing, connected, reconnecting, disconnected, and closed.
        ✔ Preserve the last received event ID and reconnect using the SSE client's standard reconnection behavior.
        ✔ Make leader election safe when multiple tabs claim leadership at the same time.
        ✔ Coordinate tenant and stream scopes consistently across leader and follower tabs.
        ✔ Add explicit `close()` cleanup for timers, page listeners, BroadcastChannel, and EventSource.
        ✔ Stop attempting reconnects after the SDK has been intentionally closed.
        ✔ Add clear handling for missing browser APIs and initialization failures.
        ✔ Add named and default package exports that work in ESM, CommonJS, UMD, and browser CDN usage.
    Complete React integration [Frontend]:
        ✔ Return stable React state from `usePravah` so consumers rerender when the SDK or connection state changes.
        ✔ Prevent stale event callbacks and unnecessary SDK recreation on equivalent options.
        ✔ Cover `usePravahState`, listener cleanup, reconnects, and unmount behavior with tests.
        ✔ Align the React examples with the implemented public API and returned values.
    Add automated coverage [QA]:
        ✔ Add Go unit tests for token validation, authorization, retention, identifier validation, and SSE serialization.
        ✔ Add Go integration tests for handshake, registration, publish, subscribe, replay, tenant isolation, cleanup, and health endpoints.
        ✔ Add SDK tests for leader election, cross-tab delivery, reconnect token refresh, and cleanup.
        ✔ Add tests proving the SDK keeps one active EventSource across multiple browser tabs.
        ✔ Add load tests for the agreed concurrent-connection and publication targets.
        ✔ Add security tests for invalid API keys, unauthorized streams, oversized payloads, and CORS behavior.
    Finalize deployment and operations [DevOps]:
        ✔ Add a Compose profile or command that starts only the supported MVP service and MongoDB.
        ✔ Pin MongoDB to the 9.0 release line, configure its data path, and keep host ports unpublished.
        ✔ Add container health checks for the Go service and MongoDB dependency.
        ☐ Add CI for Go formatting, vetting, tests, SDK tests, and SDK build.
        ☐ Publish versioned, non-root container images to GHCR or Docker Hub.
        ☐ Add release versioning and changelog conventions across the service and SDK.
        ✔ Document backup, retention, secret rotation, upgrade, and rollback procedures.
    Improve developer experience [Developer Experience]:
        ✔ Publish a focused OpenAPI specification for registration, health, publishing, and subscription.
        ✔ Add a copy-paste quickstart that starts Pravah, registers an app, publishes a signal, and displays it.
        ☐ Add a local playground showing connection status, active streams, and incoming signals.
        ☐ Publish `pravah-sdk` to npm with verified package metadata and supported-runtime documentation.
        ☐ Provide React, vanilla JavaScript, curl, Go, and Node publishing examples.
        ✔ Reconcile README, developer guide, PRD, SRS, and file-structure documentation around the Go architecture.
        ✔ Add troubleshooting guidance for cookies, CORS, reverse proxies, buffering, and reconnects.
    Validate market claims [Product]:
        ✔ Benchmark active connections, publication throughput, drops, and publication latency in a reproducible environment.
        ✔ Measure one active SSE connection across multiple tabs in automated SDK tests and document the reproduction.
        ✔ Measure image size for the production container.
        ✔ Define exactly what the signal-only security guarantee does and does not protect.
        ✔ Replace unsupported superlatives with benchmark-backed positioning.
        ☐ Test the landing-page message with at least five target developers or teams.
    Prepare MVP1 launch [Marketing]:
        ☐ Publish a demo showing multiple tabs sharing one connection and receiving a resource-invalidating signal.
        ☐ Create comparison content for polling, WebSockets, hosted notification services, and self-hosted Pravah.
        ☐ Build launch pages around reliable dashboard updates, lower connection overhead, and deployment control.
        ☐ Record a short quickstart and load-test demonstration.
        ☐ Add issue templates, contribution guidance, security reporting, and a public roadmap.
        ☐ Release MVP1 with a tagged container image, npm package, quickstart, benchmark notes, and known limitations.
